{"id":"2092055394852159836","platform":"x","account":{"username":"Wise1Philosophy"},"published_at":"2026-08-25T01:04:09Z","content":"Is AI going to cure cancer? The first hard evidence came from a 1,137-patient trial. Moderna and Merck tested their personalized mRNA cancer treatment in patients whose high-risk melanoma had been surgically removed. Every one of them received a therapy built for their tumor alone. It became the first treatment of its kind to pass the final stage of clinical testing. The process starts with sequencing. Doctors read the patient&apos;s tumor DNA and compare it against their healthy DNA to find the mutations unique to the cancer. A machine learning pipeline then predicts which of those mutations are most likely to trigger an immune response. The system encodes up to 34 of the strongest targets into a custom mRNA shot that trains the immune system to hunt down whatever cancer cells remain. The earlier trial followed patients for five years. Adding the shot to Keytruda cut the risk of recurrence or death by 49% and the risk of distant spread or death by 59% compared with Keytruda alone. I&apos;ll be precise about the AI&apos;s role because this field owes people precision. The algorithm doesn&apos;t cure anything. It solves one narrow problem, choosing the right 34 targets out of thousands of candidate mutations, one patient at a time. And without that computation, the therapy couldn&apos;t exist at all. The industry spends billions convincing you AI matters because it writes your emails. Meanwhile, a model nobody posts about picks the mutations that teach a human immune system to finish off melanoma. That&apos;s the version of AI worth funding. Media","url":"https://x.com/alex_verem/status/2092055394852159836","media":[{"type":"video","source_url":"https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2092055358940577792/vid/avc1/1280x720/M-xf00r5gDm-7vdO.mp4?tag=14"}],"metadata":{"collected_at":"2026-08-25T06:54:40Z","collector":"hermes"}}
